      usedToTreat: "Learning the practical identities of common cephalosporins without assuming that a higher generation is universally stronger, safer, or broader for every organism and site.",
      description: "Cephalosporin generations describe broad historical spectrum trends, not a ladder where each step replaces the one before it. Cefazolin and cephalexin are strong first-generation choices for susceptible streptococci and MSSA; cefazolin is also a major surgical-prophylaxis drug. Cefuroxime expands selected respiratory gram-negative coverage and its oral axetil prodrug has formulation and absorption rules. Ceftriaxone offers long exposure and useful CNS and disseminated-infection roles but has neonatal bilirubin/calcium and biliary-sludge cautions. Ceftazidime emphasizes gram-negative and Pseudomonas activity but is weaker for MSSA. Cefepime combines broad gram-negative, Pseudomonas, and better gram-positive activity yet can cause renal-accumulation neurotoxicity because falling kidney clearance raises CNS exposure. Ceftaroline binds MRSA PBP2a but is not a Pseudomonas drug.",
      mechanism: "Every agent acylates PBPs and blocks peptidoglycan transpeptidation, but side chains change porin entry, PBP affinity, beta-lactamase stability, protein binding, renal or biliary handling, and tissue penetration. Those structural differences create the real bedside identities. Ceftazidime and cefepime can reach susceptible Pseudomonas through gram-negative outer-membrane pathways; ceftaroline's flexible binding interaction permits high-affinity engagement of altered MRSA PBP2a. Ceftriaxone's long half-life and high protein binding support less frequent dosing but also explain bilirubin displacement and precipitation with calcium in neonates. Cefepime clearance is predominantly renal, so accumulation can antagonize CNS GABA-A signaling and produce encephalopathy, myoclonus, or nonconvulsive status epilepticus.",
