      mechanism: "Atropine reversibly competes with acetylcholine at M1 through M5 muscarinic receptors. Cardiac M2 blockade reduces Gi signaling, restores adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, decreases acetylcholine-activated potassium current, and steepens phase-4 pacemaker depolarization. M3 blockade reduces glandular secretion and relaxes selected smooth muscle, explaining dry mouth, bronchodilation, urinary retention, impaired accommodation, and reduced sweating. It does not block nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction. In organophosphate exposure, dose is titrated to drying of dangerous pulmonary secretions and improved ventilation rather than pupil size or a memorized small maximum.",
