      description: "Amoxicillin is an orally absorbed aminopenicillin beta-lactam that binds bacterial penicillin-binding proteins and blocks peptidoglycan cross-linking, killing susceptible actively growing organisms. Its amino side chain improves entry into selected gram-negative bacteria, while better oral absorption than ampicillin supports outpatient use. It covers many susceptible streptococci, Enterococcus faecalis, and selected non-beta-lactamase-producing respiratory gram-negative organisms, but it does not reliably cover MRSA, Pseudomonas, atypical organisms, or beta-lactamase-producing bacteria unless an appropriate inhibitor such as clavulanate addresses that enzyme. A familiar drug is not automatically the correct drug for every sore throat or ear symptom.",
      mechanism: "After absorption, free amoxicillin reaches extracellular fluid and acylates transpeptidase PBPs. The final peptide cross-links in bacterial peptidoglycan cannot form, while existing autolysins continue remodeling the wall; osmotic rupture follows during growth. Beta-lactamases hydrolyze the beta-lactam ring before PBP binding, altered PBPs lower affinity, and porin or efflux changes reduce access. Clavulanate irreversibly inhibits selected serine beta-lactamases but does not restore activity against every ESBL, AmpC, carbapenemase, altered PBP, or intrinsically resistant organism. Predominant renal elimination explains longer exposure and seizure risk when severe kidney dysfunction is not reflected in dosing.",
